parts.text:
Hmm, `commit()` in a loop is bad — it writes to disk on every call, and the loop as written is still racy (read-modify-write without CAS). Let me do this properly with an in-process AtomicLong guarded, and only persist periodically. Actually SharedPreferences doesn't have CAS.

Better approach: keep an AtomicLong in EnginePrefs (process-level single instance), synchronized.

```kotlin
private val tokenLock = Any()
private val tokenCache = java.util.concurrent.atomic.AtomicLong(-1)

fun incrementTokens(ctx: Context, plus: Long) {
    if (plus <= 0) return
    val prefs = sp(ctx)
    var total: Long
    synchronized(tokenLock) {
        var cur = tokenCache.get()
        if (cur < 0) { cur = prefs.getLong("token_usage", 0L); tokenCache.set(cur) }
        total = cur + plus
        tokenCache.set(total)
    }
    prefs.edit().putLong("token_usage", total).apply()
}

fun tokenUsageCount(ctx: Context): Long {
    val cur = tokenCache.get()
    if (cur >= 0) return cur
    val v = sp(ctx).getLong("token_usage", 0L)
    tokenCache.set(v)
    return v
}
```

That's correct within the process, cheap, and avoids the infinite-loop commit. Let me look at the filefirst.

